Base Score: 5.1 (Medium)

Depending on organizational standards, this issue may also be classified as Low because exploitation requires local access and user interaction.

Vulnerability Category

Code Signing / Binary Integrity Weakness

OWASP Category
OWASP Desktop Application Security Verification Standard (ASVS)
V14 – Configuration
V1 – Architecture, Design and Threat Modeling
CWE
CWE-347 – Improper Verification of Cryptographic Signature
CWE-494 – Download of Code Without Integrity Check
Description

During the assessment of the thick client application (prime.exe), the Sysinternals Sigcheck utility was used to inspect the executable's digital signature. It was observed that the application binary located at:

C:\Program Files\EidosMedia\Methode 6.0\bin\prime.exe

is not digitally signed.

The Sigcheck output reported:

Verified: Unsigned
Publisher: n/a

Digital signatures provide authenticity and integrity verification of executable files and ensure that binaries originate from a trusted publisher and have not been tampered with. The absence of code signing prevents users and security tools from verifying the legitimacy of the executable and increases the risk of malware substitution or unauthorized modification.

Impact

An attacker with access to the system may:

Replace the executable with a malicious version without signature validation warnings.
Tamper with application binaries without detection.
Distribute trojanized versions of the application.
Reduce trustworthiness and integrity assurance of software updates.
Increase the likelihood of supply-chain and binary replacement attacks.
Mitigation
Digitally sign all application binaries using a trusted Code Signing Certificate.
Implement signature verification during application deployment and updates.
Enforce integrity checks before executing binaries.
Maintain secure software release and distribution processes.
Periodically validate digital signatures during patch management and software inventory reviews.
