Vulnerability Heading

Internal Configuration Path Disclosure in Thick Client Application

Severity

Low

CVSS v3.1

CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N

Base Score: 3.1 (Low)

Vulnerability Category

Information Disclosure

OWASP Category
OWASP Top 10 2021

A05:2021 – Security Misconfiguration

OWASP Desktop Application Security

Configuration and Error Handling Weakness

CWE
CWE-209 – Information Exposure Through an Error Message
CWE-497 – Exposure of System Data to an Unauthorized Control Sphere
Description

During testing of the thick client application, an error message was observed that disclosed internal application configuration paths. When a file loading operation failed, the application displayed the complete internal path structure:

/SysConfig/Research2/SiteConfig/01_DigitalDesign/PageAccessories/AccessoriesConfig/...

This information reveals the application's internal directory hierarchy and configuration naming conventions to authenticated users. Such disclosures provide valuable information that may aid attackers in understanding the application architecture and conducting further targeted attacks.

Impact

An attacker may leverage the disclosed information to:

Enumerate internal directory structures.
Identify configuration repositories and resource locations.
Facilitate path traversal or file inclusion attacks.
Gain insight into the application's architecture.
Improve the effectiveness of subsequent attacks.

Although no direct exploitation was identified, exposing internal implementation details increases the attack surface and violates the principle of least information disclosure.

Mitigation
Avoid exposing internal paths and system details in user-facing error messages.
Return generic error messages such as:
Unable to load the requested resource.
Please contact the administrator.
Log detailed errors only on the server side.
Implement centralized exception handling.
Sanitize error responses before presenting them to users.
Disable verbose debugging information in production environments.
